(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old man is farsighted. Why does a convex lens correct him?","choices":["It magnifies the retinal image","It filters ultraviolet light","It converges light onto the retina","It creates several focal points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His eye focuses behind the retina. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Two patients have uveal melanoma, one of the iris and one of the ciliary body. What do you expect?","choices":["Both are almost always fatal","The iris tumour carries the better outlook","The ciliary body tumour does better","The outlook is identical"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, roughly 4 to 10 percent mortality against far worse. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had cataract surgery three years ago and reports gradual clouding again. What treats this?","choices":["Anti-VEGF injection","New spectacles alone","Laser to the clouded posterior capsule","Repeat lens extraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Residual epithelial cells opacify it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has two years of gradual blurring, glare at night and a dimmed red reflex. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Cataract","Macular degeneration","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow blurring, glare and a dulled reflex. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A father asks why his son must wear a patch over his good eye. What do you explain?","choices":["It makes the weaker eye work","It rests the strained good eye","It corrects the misalignment mechanically","It prevents infection spreading"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stronger eye otherwise does everything. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a suspected uveal melanoma. What is fine needle aspiration used for here?","choices":["To confirm the diagnosis before treatment","To deliver chemotherapy","To relieve raised pressure","Prognostic molecular testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, rather than to make the diagnosis. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with diabetes has never had an eye examination. What do you arrange?","choices":["Optometry for a refraction","Review only if vision changes","No follow-up while she sees well","Annual ophthalmology review"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A medical eye risk needs the medical specialist. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has a congenital ptosis that has covered her pupil since birth. What is the visual risk?","choices":["Deprivation amblyopia","Glaucoma","Retinoblastoma","Macular degeneration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An obstructed view stops the eye developing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a flat, 2 mm, avascular pigmented iris spot noticed at a routine visit. What do you do?","choices":["Refer for urgent oncology assessment","Start surveillance imaging of the liver","Excise it in clinic","Refer to ophthalmology to document and monitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Likely benign, but a baseline is needed. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"An 86-year-old man has a dense scar at the macula from previous bleeding. Which form of macular degeneration is this?","choices":["Neither; scarring is unrelated","Dry, because it is now stable","Wet only if it is still bleeding","Wet, because it began with neovascular bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The scar is the end stage of the wet process. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has painless loss of peripheral vision over years, cupped discs and a raised pressure. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Macular degeneration","Cataract","Primary open-angle glaucoma","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with central acuity spared until late. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with early dry macular degeneration smokes 20 a day. What is the most useful advice?","choices":["Stop reading in the evening","Stop smoking","Take beta carotene at high dose","Reduce dietary salt"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the risk factor he can change. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has an iris lesion and asks how it will be told from a cancer. What distinguishes them?","choices":["Whether it affects vision","Growth, size and vascularity","Which eye it is in","Whether it is painful"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nevus stays flat, small and avascular. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man asks why he lost side vision without noticing. Why does open-angle glaucoma go unnoticed?","choices":["It causes halos that mask the loss","It is always preceded by injury","It is painless and spares central vision early","It only affects one eye at a time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why screening exists. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a cataract but manages daily life without difficulty. What do you advise?","choices":["Repeat glasses every three months","Defer until it affects daily life","Operate now to prevent worsening","Refer urgently to ophthalmology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, unless another eye disease forces the issue. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman develops horizontal double vision that resolves when either eye is covered. What is the next step?","choices":["Patch one eye permanently","Assess ocular alignment and cranial nerves","Prescribe reading glasses","Reassure and review in six months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Binocular diplopia means misalignment until proven otherwise. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a cataract and uveitis in the same eye. How urgent is referral?","choices":["No referral is needed","Urgent only if pressure is raised","Urgent, given the uveitis","Routine, as cataract is never urgent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An isolated cataract is a routine referral. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"You suspect retinoblastoma in a 2-year-old and the offered appointment is in two months. What do you do?","choices":["Arrange imaging and review the results first","Call the specialist and get it brought forward","Accept the appointment offered","Refer to a different specialty instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and document it if they will not. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with a normal eye examination, normal pressure and a clear lens reports profound vision loss that does not fit any pattern. What should be considered?","choices":["Open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Functional visual loss","Early cataract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, once organic causes have been excluded. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman is confirmed to have wet macular degeneration. Which treatment is offered?","choices":["Patching the affected eye","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection","The AREDS2 supplement alone","Pressure-lowering drops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with photodynamic therapy and laser as alternatives. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a cataract and cannot have her retina examined because of it. Does that change the plan?","choices":["Yes, surgery is needed to see behind it","No, wait until vision is worse","No, imaging can substitute entirely","Yes, but only if she has glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It blocks diagnosis of other eye disease. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 84-year-old man with dry macular degeneration is given a way to check himself at home. Which is it?","choices":["An Amsler grid","A near vision card","A pinhole occluder","A colour vision plate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Distortion of the grid signals change. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with obesity describes a whooshing in her ears in time with her pulse, plus headache and brief greyouts. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Giant cell arteritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulsatile tinnitus with transient visual loss is characteristic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a dome-shaped pigmented mass under the retina found on routine fundoscopy, and no symptoms. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Choroidal nevus","Choroidal haemangioma","Retinoblastoma","Choroidal melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most are found incidentally and are asymptomatic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is told her eyeball is long. What does that predict about her vision?","choices":["Distance blurred, near clear","Near blurred, distance clear","Blur at every distance","Loss of colour vision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myopia focuses distant objects in front of the retina. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a raised, vascular pigmented conjunctival lesion that has enlarged. Which diagnosis is most concerning?","choices":["Conjunctival nevus","Racial melanosis","Pinguecula","Conjunctival melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raised, vascular and growing. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has new headache with papilledema and no trauma. Which test is ordered first?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain","Non-contrast computed tomography","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Visual field testing alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mass has to be excluded before anything else. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with glaucoma asks why he must keep using drops when he sees no better. What do you say?","choices":["They protect the vision he still has","They will restore the lost field","They can be stopped once pressure normalises","They treat the cataract as well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Damage already done cannot be reversed. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has one eye that does not follow a toy. What is the next step?","choices":["Reassure that he will grow out of it","Patch the affected eye","Review in a year","Refer to ophthalmology for full assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Untreated misalignment leads to amblyopia. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A family asks what happens if retinoblastoma goes untreated. What is the answer?","choices":["It only spreads after ten years","It is almost always fatal","It causes blindness but not death","It becomes benign with time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why referral cannot wait. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
